Inscribed rectangle with left edge at x = 0 and right edge at x = t has height bounded by t \leq y \leq 9 - \frac{11}{3}t^{2}
Maximal height for this t is 9 - \frac{11}{3}t^{2} - t, so area A(t) = 9t - t^{2} - \frac{11}{3}t^{3}
Differentiate to get A\prime(t) = 9 - 2t - 11t^{2} and set to zero
Solve 11t^{2} + 2t - 9 = 0 to get t = \frac{-2 + 20}{22} = \frac{9}{11} as the positive root
Evaluate A(\frac{9}{11}) = \frac{29403 - 2673 - 8019}{3993} = \frac{567}{121}
This equals option C → (C)

30842:
Differentiate both sides in x: the integral contributes 10(x + 2)f(x) by the fundamental theorem
Left derivative is 4(x + 2)f(x) + 2(x + 2)^{2}f\prime(x) - 6(x + 2)
Cancelling against the right side leaves 2(x + 2)f\prime(x) = 6f(x) + 6
Separate to get \frac{f\prime(x)}{f(x) + 1} = \frac{3}{x + 2}, integrating to \ln|f(x) + 1| = 3\ln(x + 2) + C_{1}
Hence f(x) + 1 = C(x + 2)^{3}; setting x = 0 in the original gives f(0) = \frac{3}{2} so C = \frac{5}{16}
At x = 2 get f(2) + 1 = \frac{5}{16} \cdot 64 = 20, so f(2) = 19 → (19)

30843:
Use \sec^{2}\theta = 1 + \tan^{2}\theta with \theta = \tan^{-1}\alpha to get first term 1 + \alpha^{2}
Use \text{cosec}^{2}\phi = 1 + \cot^{2}\phi with \phi = \cot^{-1}\beta to get second term 1 + \beta^{2}
Sum condition gives \alpha^{2} + \beta^{2} = 34
Square \alpha + \beta = 8 to get 64 = 34 + 2\alpha\beta, so \alpha\beta = 15
Numbers with sum 8 and product 15 are 3 and 5; with \alpha \leq \beta take \alpha = 3 and \beta = 5
Then \alpha^{2} + \beta = 9 + 5 = 14 → (14)
